THE CACHE
=========

A key-value store in front of the database, so that the same question is
not put to PostgreSQL on every connection and every nick change.

The one thing to understand before anything else:

  **The cache is never the truth.**

Everything that reads it reads the database too.  A cache that is missing,
empty, stale, slow or down costs a query and nothing else: there is no
degraded mode to design for, no failover, and no error a caller has to
handle specially.  That is why cache_get() returning zero -- which is what
happens when no cache module is loaded, or ircd.conf has no Redis{} block
-- needs no branch of its own.  It is the same thing as a miss.

What does matter is the database not answering, and that is db.h's to
report.


THE SHAPE
=========

The same as the database driver, for the same two reasons: modules are
RTLD_LOCAL and cannot resolve each other's symbols, so the core has to be
the meeting point between whoever caches and whoever implements the cache;
and holding the calls in flight in the core is what lets the driver module
be unloaded with some still outstanding.

  include/cache.h       the API, the Redis{} block, the driver interface
  ircd/cache.c          the register, the calls in flight, the config
  modules/workers/redis the driver

The core knows nothing about Redis.  It knows there is a store with keys,
values and an expiry, and that it may not be there.


USING IT
========

From the core:

  cache_get(NULL, "nick:maria", on_result, data);
  cache_set(NULL, "nick:maria", json, len, 300, NULL, NULL);
  cache_del(NULL, "nick:maria", NULL, NULL);

From a module, with its handle so its calls are dropped if it unloads:

  module_cache_get(mod, "nick:maria", on_result, data);
  module_cache_set(mod, "nick:maria", json, len, 300, NULL, NULL);
  module_cache_del(mod, "nick:maria", NULL, NULL);

The answer arrives in the main thread:

  static void on_result(const struct CacheResult *res, void *user)
  {
    if (res->cres_code != CACHE_OK || !res->cres_hit) {
      ask_the_database();      /* a miss and a failure are the same path */
      return;
    }
    use(res->cres_value, res->cres_len);
  }

Nothing in the result outlives the call: copy what is needed.


THE PATTERN
===========

Read through, write around, invalidate on write:

  1. Ask the cache.
  2. On a hit, answer from it.
  3. On a miss -- or on any failure -- ask the database, answer, and write
     what was found back to the cache.
  4. **Cache the misses too.**  "This nickname is not registered" is the
     majority answer, and a cache that only holds hits leaves most of the
     traffic reaching the database anyway, which is what it was put there
     to stop.
  5. After writing to the database, cache_del() the keys that changed.
     Do not wait for the expiry: the store is shared, so deleting a key on
     one server deletes it for all of them, and the alternative is every
     other server serving a stale answer until the clock runs out.  The
     TTL is the net under whatever is written outside the server, not the
     mechanism.


VALUES
======

Opaque bytes to the core, and binary-safe end to end: a value may contain
NUL.  What actually goes in them is JSON, with jansson, because the things
worth caching have more than one field and a string with separators in it
is a format that will need versioning one day.

  CACHE_KEY_MAX     255 bytes, prefix included
  CACHE_VALUE_MAX   64 KiB -- bigger belongs in the database
  CACHE_TTL_DEFAULT 300 seconds when a caller asks for none
  CACHE_TTL_MAX     one day; an entry that outlives the server that wrote
                    it is one nobody remembers the reason for

The prefix from the Redis{} block is applied by the core, once, on the way
in.  A driver that had to remember to do it would be a driver that one day
forgot, and two networks sharing a store would read each other's keys.


THE Redis{} BLOCK
=================

  Redis {
    host = "127.0.0.1";     # or: socket = "/run/redis/redis.sock";
    port = 6379;
    password = "${IRCU_REDIS_PASSWORD}";
    database = 0;
    pool = 2;
    timeout_ms = 200;       # or: timeout = 1 seconds;
    prefix = "ircu:";
  };

  Module { name = "redis"; };

Either host or socket is required; everything else has a default.  A
timeout over two seconds is corrected down with a warning: a cache slower
than that is not a cache, because the point of asking it first is that it
answers before the database would.

The driver needs worker threads -- a connection is a thread -- so
WORKER_THREADS must be at least the pool size.  With workers off the
module loads and refuses every call, which costs a database query and
nothing else.

Taking the block out and rehashing stops the cache being used.  The driver
keeps whatever connections it has rather than dropping them mid-call, but
cache_available() is false without a block, so nothing new is asked.


WRITING A DRIVER
================

Register a struct CacheDriver with module_add_cache_driver().  Its three
entry points start the work and return; the answer goes back later, in the
main thread, with cache_complete(), exactly once per call accepted.

  * One driver at a time.  A second registration is refused rather than
    replacing the first.
  * Never block the main thread.  The driver in this tree uses hiredis
    synchronously in a worker thread per connection, which is the simplest
    thing that works: an event-driven client would have to be woven into
    the server's own event loop, and what that would avoid -- the main
    thread waiting on a socket -- the worker threads already avoid.
  * A call the core no longer holds is not an error to answer:
    cache_complete() says so and does nothing.
  * The core has its own deadline and will fail a call the driver never
    answers, so a driver that loses one costs a query, not a hang.


STATISTICS
==========

  cache_calls_total()    calls made
  cache_calls_hit()      of those, how many found the key
  cache_calls_failed()   of those, how many failed

A miss is neither a hit nor a failure.  It is an answer.
